WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:05.923
- [Sal] We're live.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:14.253
Stand by.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:27.220
Hi everyone, welcome to the
Daily Homeroom live stream.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.530
Sorry we're running a little bit late.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:31.680
As you can see, I am in
a different location.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.710
I think I finally got the
WiFi fixed in the house.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:35.790
So we're gonna try this,

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.930
you might hear some screaming
kids every now and then,

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.040
but that just'll add a little bit

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.380
of flavor to the experience.

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:45.020
Exciting show today, but before
we get into the thick of it,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.210
I will explain for those
of you all who might

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.610
just be showing up for the
first time what this thing is.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.150
Khan Academy, we're not for profit,

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:53.730
and our mission is to provide a free,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.190
world-class education
for anyone, anywhere,

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.220
and when we saw, it seems
like a lifetime ago,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.290
but now five, six, seven weeks ago,

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.080
that we had these mass school closures,

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.660
not just in the US but around the world,

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.250
we saw given our mission,

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.810
given that we've been
creating content and tools

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.880
to support parents, teachers, students

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.090
for the last 10 years,

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:15.200
that's it's really our
duty to step up even more

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.540
during this crisis,

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.810
and so above and beyond
all the learning materials

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.620
that'll hopefully keep folks learning,

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.040
we've been trying to do parent
webinars, teacher webinars,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.970
this live stream, just as
a way to stay in touch.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.320
As we go through the very
exciting conversation today,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.030
I encourage you put questions

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:32.780
for either myself or our guest,

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.940
who's a super teacher, on
either Facebook or YouTube.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:38.950
We have team members who
are looking at those.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.060
I do like to remind folks Khan Academy,

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.430
as I said, is a not-for-profit.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:44.790
We exist because of
philanthropic donations.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.640
If you're in a position to do so,

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.610
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.100
I do want to give special thanks

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.110
to the several corporate
partners who've stepped up

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.360
in the last few weeks to
allow us to do this work.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.090
We've been running at a
deficit even before the crisis,

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.130
and you can imagine
our costs have gone up.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:02.810
We're seeing three times the server load

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:03.740
that we typically see,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.080
and we're trying to
accelerate a lot of programs,

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.550
so special thanks to Bank of America,

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.817
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly, Novartis,

00:02:11.817 --> 00:02:13.120
and we need more help,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.480
so anyone out there who
wants to help us out more,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.710
either as an individual
donor or as a corporation,

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.200
please reach out.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.033
So with that,

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:23.550
would love to get into
the core of the program,

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.150
I'd love to introduce Conor Corey,

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.900
who's an incredible teacher

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.200
from the Centennial School
District in Pennsylvania.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.873
Conor, great to have you here.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.180
- Thanks, Sal, I'm excited to be here.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.310
- And first of all,

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.120
this is the launch of
Teacher Appreciation Week,

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.550
so let me just tell you,

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.710
I think we all appreciated
teachers before this,

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:47.410
or hopefully we should be,

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:48.670
and you're a super teacher

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.700
who we've been appreciating
for many years,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.720
and I think for many of us parents who now

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.310
have to handle our own kids in our house,

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.060
the level of teacher appreciation
has gone up (laughs).

00:03:01.162 --> 00:03:01.995
- (laughs) I agree--

00:03:01.995 --> 00:03:02.828
- Thank you so much.

00:03:02.828 --> 00:03:05.760
- I've been getting many
positive emails from parents

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.610
that appreciate the
help as they're confused

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:11.223
and stressed out as many
of us are during this time.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.710
- Yeah, so maybe a good place to start,

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:18.530
you're what's known as a Khan
Academy Teacher Ambassador.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:22.160
Tell us a little bit about what you teach,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:23.110
tell us about your kids,

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:26.120
tell us about how you've
been using Khan Academy

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.333
and what this means to be an ambassador.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:33.660
- So this is my 17th year teaching,

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.262
and I spent the majority
of my career teaching

00:03:37.262 --> 00:03:39.240
with the Centennial
Public School District,

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.630
and that's where I really
discovered Khan Academy.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:47.540
We were pretty under-funded
and many of our students,

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.300
they didn't have a learning disability,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.010
but they were maybe a year
or a few years behind,

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.400
and Khan Academy always
gave me the ability

00:03:55.439 --> 00:03:58.240
to remediate content without
having to fill out paperwork

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.780
or go find a different book,

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.260
but it was always free academic content,

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.960
and from there I continued making it

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.720
a larger and larger
part of the curriculum,

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.350
and when the Ambassador Program
came out a few years ago,

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.970
I applied to that and it's been fantastic.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:17.840
There's, right now we have a thousand,

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.370
over a thousand people
that donate their time

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:21.420
to help other teachers,

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.040
to show them just different ways to access

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:26.640
a variety of education.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.660
When I first started, it was just math,

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:32.100
but it has become so much more and just,

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.850
I know parents that are using
it in high school, college,

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.220
it's really been a huge help

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:42.080
for not only the education
system, but for the world,

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.280
so I mean I've always fully
supported this program,

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:48.617
maybe not financially but
with my time (laughs).

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:51.440
- The way that you're supporting it

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:52.710
is as essential as anything,

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.730
and we really consider you a close part

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:57.210
of this Khan Academy team.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.398
I think one of the things that is really,

00:04:59.398 --> 00:05:02.970
you know you stood out as an
ambassador for Khan Academy.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.100
Obviously we've put those
resources out there,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.010
and we have efficacy
studies that if students

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:08.490
are able to put in the time

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:09.770
they're able to accelerate their learning,

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.030
but the key question that we've
been trying to answer this,

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.156
many people have been asking this

00:05:14.156 --> 00:05:15.417
in this live stream and other places is,

00:05:15.417 --> 00:05:17.460
"Well, how do we motivate kids to do it?"

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.960
Especially they're at home now,

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.300
it's not the same as being in a classroom,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:25.470
what were your techniques before COVID

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.240
and then what tips do you
have for parents and teachers

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.440
and even students themselves

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:31.503
to stay motivated during this crisis?

00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:37.650
- Motivation's always been fun
for me while I was teaching,

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.150
I always looked at it as,

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.450
our students are so drastically different

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:42.690
and come from different backgrounds.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.850
I could buy an Xbox
and put that as a prize

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:48.570
but then there's a kid in the
class that has two Xboxes,

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:49.990
that doesn't motivate him,

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.910
so I always found that
embarrassing yourself

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:54.260
as an adult for a child

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.600
or a reward they can't purchase

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.240
is something that will
motivate all of them,

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.170
so over the years during LearnStorm

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:04.990
we've had kids throw water balloons at us,

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.190
we did something called the Wheel of Doom,

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:11.480
where we had 26 teachers sign
up and the winning class,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:14.010
each kid spun this wheel
and whatever it landed on,

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.340
ketchup, mustard, mayonnaise,

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:16.210
we had a whole assembly,

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:19.640
they were able to squirt
that in the teacher's face.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:21.820
All for kind of laughs.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:25.200
We had even one teacher
and our vice principal

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.290
let us wax their leg with
wax strips as a reward.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:29.187
- Wow.

00:06:29.187 --> 00:06:32.707
- We were running out of ideas
as you could tell (laughs).

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:35.630
- You are a dedicated teacher.

00:06:38.168 --> 00:06:39.200
- In school it's always,

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.740
I have the sense of humor
of a middle school kid, so--

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.700
- Or maybe waxing your legs
would not be quite as painful

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.647
as mine, I am, anyway (laughs).

00:06:47.841 --> 00:06:52.240
- I don't know, it's pretty bad (laughs).

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.106
- Just extrapolate from my beard,

00:06:54.106 --> 00:06:56.160
and you know what my legs
look like, sorry TMI.

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:02.213
No that's fascinating, keep going.

00:07:03.986 --> 00:07:06.160
- It's just all about finding
out how we're just trying

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.640
to do the same things
and trying to keep it,

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.600
that interaction with the students.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.440
Yesterday they finished,

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:15.980
we timed how many,

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:17.260
many of the parents were nervous

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:19.350
they weren't prepared for 7th grade math.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:20.830
I teach 6th grade math.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:22.890
So they're very nervous with
everything that's happened,

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:26.840
so I spent a week and only
assigned them growth mindset

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.930
activities that we did earlier in the year

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.660
during LearnStorm.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.910
I wanted to reinforce that,
especially at this time,

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.500
and then last week I gave them some

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.270
7th grade assignments
without much teaching.

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:43.237
I was like, "You have
the background knowledge,

00:07:43.237 --> 00:07:45.057
"I want you to try these
things a few times,

00:07:45.057 --> 00:07:46.347
"remember you're going to get them wrong,

00:07:46.347 --> 00:07:48.937
"remember to practice
what we did last week

00:07:48.937 --> 00:07:49.850
"with growth mindset."

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:51.443
If you have--

00:07:51.443 --> 00:07:55.693
(garbled audio drowns out speaker)

00:07:57.874 --> 00:07:59.820
I would do the Blender Challenge,

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:03.770
and I would blend up
seven things in my fridge

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:06.340
and drink it on camera for them,

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.320
and we had parents sign up also,

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:11.400
'cause I told them to
sign their parents up,

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.880
but today I've watched
eight different videos

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.170
of parents laughing with their kids,

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:20.810
blend up disgusting things
in the fridge, all for math,

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:22.750
just to kind of get them laughing again

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.160
instead of just sitting around.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:26.820
We've been trying to do
stupid stuff like that

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.800
and I had a student
now giving me requests.

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:31.440
I have two rules.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.010
It has to be free and legal,

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.147
those are my only two rules
and I'll do it (laughs).

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:38.133
- That's very, I love that,

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:40.370
because we always talk about motivation,

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:42.170
we usually think it has to be some deep,

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:44.460
psychological mind trick
that you play with kids,

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:49.460
or maybe we do defer to the
not-as-healthy of rewards,

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:55.050
like here's some sugar, here's
some not-so-good screen time,

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:57.040
but what you're saying,
this is really fun,

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.700
it's these memorable, quirky,

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.590
you just have to have a
little bit of a willingness

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:04.550
to subject yourself to
the whim of a child.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:06.360
You accomplish this,

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:09.140
I as you parent or as
your teacher am willing

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:13.600
to do what you want,
something fun, quirky,

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.203
as long as it's legal and no
one gets hurt, so to speak.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:20.550
- Yeah, that a pretty
tough (garbled speech).

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.400
- And related to that, this
first question from Facebook,

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.197
Scott Yang asks, "Hi, Conor.

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:26.930
"How does it feel to teach from home?"

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.940
So you described what
you were doing pre-COVID.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:30.950
I am curious,

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:33.320
I think a lot of teachers
and parents are curious,

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:34.880
so how are you keeping that same,

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.970
or can you keep that same level of energy

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:38.630
or connectedness with
your students right now,

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:40.130
how are you trying to do that?

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:42.970
- I'm trying to,

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:47.080
I don't believe it's
possible to do the same way,

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:50.530
because I can't see my
student's face live as I'm,

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.620
I'm kind of recording my
lessons and sending them

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.380
and answering questions when
they record something back,

00:09:56.380 --> 00:09:58.130
but I know when I'm in class

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:01.690
when Jacob's nodding and I'm talking,

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:02.933
he has no idea what I'm talking about,

00:10:02.933 --> 00:10:05.060
and I just have to step in there.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:08.970
But I can see when one student doesn't sit

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:10.590
with the friends they usually sit with

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.580
that maybe they're having a bad day

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:13.417
and something's going on,

00:10:13.417 --> 00:10:15.870
and as teachers we're
aware of those things,

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:17.090
and we really try to focus

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:19.543
on that social piece just as much.

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:23.807
It's very difficult this way, nowadays.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:29.510
I'm fortunate enough to
still have my employment,

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:31.740
but many of our parents have lost that,

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.430
and there's kids,

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:35.860
I have four children of my
own throughout the house,

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.040
I can't believe they
haven't interrupted us yet,

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:40.250
but they are usually,

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:43.130
we're trying to get everything organized

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:46.830
while I'm teaching as well
and sharing the devices,

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:50.540
so it is difficult and it's high stress

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:55.200
and I think as parents, we
need to give ourselves a break

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.950
from making sure every
little thing is done,

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:03.950
and not to cause more stress
than learning during this time.

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:07.284
But I'm trying to do my best.

00:11:07.284 --> 00:11:10.310
I don't have all the
answers, I can tell you that.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:11.850
- How are you handling, personally,

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:15.490
I mean being able to keep
your students engaged,

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:16.840
your full-time job,

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.020
trying to teach virtually however you can,

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.790
at the same time four kids at home,

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:24.480
how are you able to
navigate that personally?

00:11:26.912 --> 00:11:28.510
- Personally, I think I'm doing okay.

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:31.360
We have a great family,
I have a loving wife,

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:32.193
she's very supportive,

00:11:32.193 --> 00:11:36.800
and I try and keep my mindset
and find the positives

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.450
in things that are going on,

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:40.740
and the positives of
getting this much time

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:42.850
with my children and my family

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:47.850
and to really focus on that
rather than the many negatives

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:50.886
that you can see on the
news and the negative things

00:11:50.886 --> 00:11:53.010
that are going on here in Pennsylvania,

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:56.220
as we have severely high cases of COVID,

00:11:56.220 --> 00:12:00.170
and you hear stories of
grandparents and parents

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:03.470
and hospitals that are overrun

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:06.000
and people are unfortunately passing away.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.830
I think it's a good learning opportunity

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:13.810
for my children to work on ourselves

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:16.010
and make sure that we're just trying

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:19.924
to stay upbeat and to give yourself time.

00:12:19.924 --> 00:12:22.430
I mean it's okay to meditate
and give yourself 20 minutes

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:24.320
to think before your day starts.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.440
It's very helpful,

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.543
and I think that helps people
get through the stress.

