which is unfortunate,

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:04.230
maybe we'll be able to
bring you on a little bit,

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:06.150
I think it just got a little bit worse,

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:08.730
but what we'll do is, I'll wind down here,

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:11.057
I'll tell everyone, "Please
feel free to ask questions

00:15:11.057 --> 00:15:13.100
"for either myself or Conor,"

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:16.300
and I'll go into a little bit
of a Ask Me Anything mode,

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:20.760
but Conor if your internet is not choppy,

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:25.760
any parting thoughts for folks
on how to teach or learn,

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:27.690
maybe what message would
you have for students

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:29.950
in this environment
who are trying to learn

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:32.300
and at the same time deal
with everything else?

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:36.047
- I think trying to learn
in this environment,

00:15:36.047 --> 00:15:40.130
you really have to
focus on growth mindset,

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:41.610
and for our older students

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:44.400
to look through Khan
Academy's career section,

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:48.950
I think that is a fantastic
way to see a linear path

00:15:48.950 --> 00:15:51.827
of what you possibly want
to do after high school

00:15:51.827 --> 00:15:53.810
and those steps for them,

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:58.810
and for them to see the steps
they need to take is valuable,

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:02.000
to know that, what they have
to do now to achieve that,

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:04.540
and kind of the
self-learning that's going on

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:10.060
all over the globe currently.

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:12.600
- Yeah, now Conor, thank you so much,

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:16.030
and I hope we can
continue this conversation

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:20.300
when the internet gods (laughs)
give us better conditions.

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:22.063
I've been there myself many times,

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:24.423
so thank you so much, Conor.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:26.603
- Thank you.

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:31.750
- So as y'all can see,

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:33.810
this is a very authentic show we do here.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:36.910
We're all dealing with the complexities

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:39.390
of being socially distanced.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:41.860
If anyone has questions,

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:46.860
I'm happy to answer them in
classic Ask Me Anything style.

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:49.740
One thing that Conor
mentioned a couple of times

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:51.710
that I'm sure a lot of
y'all are focused on

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:53.987
but it's definitely
something I think about

00:16:53.987 --> 00:16:54.820
is a growth mindset.

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:57.860
How does he give growth
mindsets to his students,

00:16:57.860 --> 00:16:59.300
or how does he even have one himself,

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.490
and that's just this
notion that you either

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:03.050
believe you're good at certain things

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:05.500
and not good at certain
things, that's a fixed mindset,

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:06.820
or you think you have a growth mindset.

00:17:06.820 --> 00:17:08.950
The only way you know what you're good at

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:11.080
is by pushing yourself and failing

00:17:11.080 --> 00:17:13.380
and reflecting on those
failures and trying again,

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:15.800
really pushing yourself
out of your comfort zone.

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:18.060
And disproportionately in any field,

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:20.470
the people who do well
aren't necessarily the people

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:21.970
who had a strong starting point.

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:23.700
They're the people who
have a growth mindset,

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.020
who are always pushing themselves
out of their comfort zone,

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:27.360
and the more you realize that,

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:29.560
the more you realize that
your brain is like a muscle,

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:31.820
the more you use it, the stronger it gets,

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:33.510
that it's actually when you fail

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:35.120
and you reflect on that failure

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.060
that you form the most neural connections.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:40.660
You actually are getting smarter
in that way when you fail,

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:45.660
then you can really start to
exercise that growth mindset,

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:47.560
so it's a super important idea

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:50.690
that we talk a lot about
in this live stream.

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:54.490
So we're getting some questions,

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:56.770
it looks like these are
for me from Facebook.

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:59.887
Qazi Aakash Ahmad asks,
"Greeting, Mr. Khan,

00:17:59.887 --> 00:18:02.967
"how does Khan Academy
help you make up your life?

00:18:02.967 --> 00:18:06.017
"I mean you have to spend
your part of life for others,

00:18:06.017 --> 00:18:07.427
"I hope it gets answered."

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:10.940
You're getting answered (laughs),

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:14.660
so that hope is coming true.

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:18.017
You know, it's I think
generous when people say,

00:18:18.017 --> 00:18:22.400
"Oh Sal, you're putting time
in your life for Khan Academy,"

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.210
but for me I consider myself

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:25.650
the luckiest person on the planet.

00:18:25.650 --> 00:18:29.270
I don't think there's a more
rewarding thing that I can do.

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:31.440
I get to work with an incredible team,

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:34.550
I get to work with incredible teachers

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:37.740
like the one that you just
saw, and administrators,

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.810
to try to help millions or
tens or hundreds of millions

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:41.930
of students over time learn,

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:45.480
so I think it's a very fortunate thing,

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:47.700
I pinch myself that I get to work on this,

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:50.680
especially considering 10
years ago, 11 years ago,

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:52.610
I was just one guy in a walk-in closet.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:55.860
The fact that Khan Academy
now has so many other people

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:58.350
being able to help and so many people

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:00.930
who are hopefully benefiting
from it makes it a huge deal.

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:04.680
In terms of balancing, I think
that's just a trick in life.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:09.210
You heard the teacher just now
talk a little bit about that,

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:10.043
but it's,

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:14.390
when you care passionately about
a mission, that's valuable,

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:16.190
but it can easily become all-encompassing,

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:18.570
so one thing that I work on in my life is

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:20.250
how do I balance these two things,

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:22.920
how do I make sure that I
always have time for my family,

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:24.140
time for my kids,

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:25.850
where my brain isn't talking,

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:28.490
thinking about the next
thing at Khan Academy,

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:29.670
the next thing to do,

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:31.650
where I can really be
present in the moment.

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:36.650
That's I think the best
way of having a balance.

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:41.537
So, from YouTube, Sebastian Ruiz asks,

00:19:41.537 --> 00:19:44.800
"How do you cope with
all the things going on?"

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:46.930
So you know, a lot of people these days

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:48.847
whenever you get on a phone
call with anyone, they're like,

00:19:48.847 --> 00:19:50.650
"Oh, well how are you
doing in the crisis?"

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:54.800
My answer has been, I think
this is hard on everyone,

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:57.630
but relatively speaking
I'm very, very lucky.

00:19:57.630 --> 00:20:02.630
I have my job, obviously my
wife is super supportive,

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:06.360
my mother-in-law lives
with us, and so together,

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:09.870
and my children's teachers
are doing heroic jobs,

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:12.260
amazing jobs at keeping our kids

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:14.420
learning and staying engaged.

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:17.680
So that combination of support
is really, really valuable

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:19.440
as we're trying to work from home,

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:22.500
and sometimes your five-year-old
might be having a tantrum.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.590
Most of the time we can kind
of have each other's backs,

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:27.870
and take care of each other.

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:30.050
I think that plus, you know,

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:32.390
I've talked about it
this live stream before,

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:33.770
I do a lot of meditation,

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:36.270
I've been doing it for
about a year-and-a-half now,

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:39.210
and the meditation really can help you

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.930
not get caught up in some of the stresses

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:46.030
that this crisis might
be putting on folks.

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:48.090
The other thing I say is try to focus

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:50.250
on what is in your zone of control,

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:53.020
what you actually can help do.

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:54.640
Sometimes you can get very overwhelmed

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:56.370
by what's going on in the broader world,

00:20:56.370 --> 00:21:00.340
so it is useful of course
to know what's going on,

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:02.760
watch the news, but don't do
too much of that (laughs),

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.220
'cause if you do too much of that,

00:21:04.220 --> 00:21:06.510
you can start to feel very
powerless and very helpless.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:08.687
So say, "What's in my zone of control?

00:21:08.687 --> 00:21:11.167
"What can I do, to quote 'Frozen 2,'

00:21:11.167 --> 00:21:13.560
"what's the next right thing to do?"

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:16.160
And just try to do that, but you know,

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:18.690
as was just said, there
is a silver lining.

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.070
I'm able to spend a lot more time

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:22.790
with my kids, with my wife.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:25.690
Between meetings I can make the bed

00:21:25.690 --> 00:21:27.860
or do some dishes or go for a walk,

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:29.660
so there is a bit of
a silver lining there,

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:31.340
I think there's some family time,

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:34.180
some down time that is
happening that otherwise,

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:37.823
it's always a go go go type of situation.

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:43.950
So from YouTube, Afton Kid 12 says,

00:21:44.397 --> 00:21:46.567
"Hey Sal, what is the best way to learn

00:21:46.567 --> 00:21:50.650
"Honors Algebra One as
a 6th grader in school?"

00:21:50.650 --> 00:21:53.790
Well what I would do is,
well there's a couple things,

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:56.920
if you're a 6th grader, I
would go to Khan Academy,

00:21:56.920 --> 00:21:58.730
and if you've already mastered 6th grade

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:00.150
or if you find it easy,

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.410
go to the 7th grade
course on Khan Academy.

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:04.390
And if you think you can
accelerate through that,

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:06.070
you can use the course challenges,

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:07.720
which you can take as
many times as you want

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:08.810
to accelerate through things.

00:22:08.810 --> 00:22:11.170
You can take unit tests
and then as you accelerate

00:22:11.170 --> 00:22:12.310
through that do 8th grade.

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:15.970
8th grade actually has a lot
of overlap with Algebra One,

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:17.530
but then go through 8th grade,

00:22:17.530 --> 00:22:20.530
and then you can go into the
Algebra One on Khan Academy.

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:21.910
Now if you feel like you're prepared,

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:24.060
you could go straight to Algebra One,

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:25.810
try to engage in those first units,

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:28.150
and if it's making sense keep going.

00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:31.050
But what I would say is,
try to find what you need,

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.290
use the course challenges and unit tests

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:34.910
to accelerate as necessary,

00:22:34.910 --> 00:22:37.900
and then the most important
thing is make a habit out of it,

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:40.130
and this is advice for
everyone regardless of whether

00:22:40.130 --> 00:22:42.470
you're tackling 3rd
grade math, Algebra One,

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:44.940
or Calculus, or Biology, whatever.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:47.000
As long as you're able to put consistently

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:50.390
20, 30, 40 minutes in a day, over time,

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:51.350
and let's say you're able to do that

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:52.900
at least four or five days a week,

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:54.120
over time you're going to see

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:56.650
that pays huge, huge, huge dividends.

00:22:56.650 --> 00:23:00.410
Other more tactical
advice I would have is,

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:02.980
focus on the practice, get that mastery,

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:05.470
but the videos are
obviously there to help you,

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:07.110
but the best way to watch videos

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:10.360
is not to watch them passively,
it's to watch them actively.

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:13.850
So think about it, if a
problem shows up in a video,

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:14.683
pause the video.

00:23:14.683 --> 00:23:15.910
Many times in the videos I make

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:17.130
I tell you to pause the video.

00:23:17.130 --> 00:23:19.490
Do that, try to work it out on your own.

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:22.390
If once, say I'm making the video,

00:23:22.390 --> 00:23:25.470
and you see it's worked
through, try to pause it,

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:27.680
see if you can do it yourself on your own.

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:29.060
And these are just good practices,

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:30.890
these are things I used to do in school

00:23:30.890 --> 00:23:34.050
where if the teacher ran through
a proof or did an example,

00:23:34.050 --> 00:23:35.070
I would either try to do it

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:36.930
before the teacher did it on the board,

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:38.090
that was very active,

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:41.160
or once they did it I would
then not look at their work

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:44.480
and try to see if I could
replicate their thought process.

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:46.790
So if you have that active
learning with the videos,

00:23:46.790 --> 00:23:48.040
you've worked on the exercises,

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:49.880
you've put that 30, 40 minutes a day,

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:50.860
you don't stop learning,

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:52.120
you keep learning through the summer,

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:53.310
I have no doubt,

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:54.960
especially given that
you're asking this question

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.850
and you seem quite eager to keep learning,

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:01.340
which is awesome, don't
lose that eagerness,

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:04.170
I think you're gonna do just fine.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:06.380
And you can also look
at some learning plans

00:24:06.380 --> 00:24:08.080
we have for Algebra One.

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.900
You can look up learning
plans, Khan Academy,

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:12.170
you'll see the one for Algebra One.

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:13.770
It can pace you between now

00:24:13.770 --> 00:24:15.650
and the start of next school year

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:17.270
to see how you can pace through that,

00:24:17.270 --> 00:24:20.020
but I'm confident that if you're motivated

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:22.750
and you put that 20, 30 minutes a day in

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:26.003
you're going to do just
fine, Afton Kid 12.

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:32.257
Qazi Ahmad from Facebook is asking,

00:24:32.257 --> 00:24:33.817
"Is there any chance for online education

00:24:33.817 --> 00:24:35.800
"to replace institutional?"

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:36.950
This is a fascinating question.

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:39.580
I've been getting a lot of
this in the press lately.

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:41.960
I think for K12 or PreK12, no.

00:24:41.960 --> 00:24:44.610
I think, you know, we just
saw an amazing teacher.

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:47.840
The types of experiences
you can get in a classroom,

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:51.190
you're not going to be able
to replicate that with online,

00:24:51.190 --> 00:24:53.970
because education isn't
just about learning

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:55.560
how to factor a polynomial

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:57.870
or learning the steps of photosynthesis.

00:24:57.870 --> 00:25:00.420
There's a whole aspect of socialization,

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:03.270
of learning to work with
others, learning to communicate,

00:25:05.187 --> 00:25:08.010
and most students need some
extra motivation, some coaching,

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:10.410
ways to get unblocked
when they get blocked.

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:11.950
There's a lot to learning that goes

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:14.580
beyond just understanding the concepts.

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:17.840
There's a lot that's going
on at a metacognitive level,

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:21.230
and so I've said this many times,

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:22.690
this is Teacher Appreciation Week,

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:24.380
so I think it's a good time to re-say it,

00:25:24.380 --> 00:25:26.770
if I had to pick between
an amazing teacher

00:25:26.770 --> 00:25:29.520
or amazing technology for my own kids,

00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:31.470
I would pick an amazing
teacher every time.

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:33.299
In fact that's why I started a school.

00:25:33.299 --> 00:25:34.930
I started Khan Academy,

00:25:34.930 --> 00:25:38.520
but I so strongly believe
in the physical experience

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:40.320
that we started a school that

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:41.790
has many amazing teachers in it,

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:44.540
so I would pick an amazing
teacher every time.

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:47.420
Now the good thing is we don't
have to make that trade-off.

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:49.560
The ideal is amazing technology

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:52.090
in support of amazing teachers,

00:25:52.090 --> 00:25:55.210
and so that's everything we
try to do at Khan Academy,

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:56.920
that's why we are so close

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:59.090
to all these amazing teacher ambassadors

00:25:59.090 --> 00:26:01.460
who give us feedback how we can be better.

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:03.580
And then support to allow those teachers

00:26:03.580 --> 00:26:07.280
to differentiate to the
needs of those 30 students,

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:08.900
to understand that every student

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:10.610
has different levels of preparedness,

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:11.860
learns at different rates,

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:14.010
and to allow students to get that,

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:17.670
which would have been very hard
as a teacher by themselves.

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:18.790
So I don't think it's a replacement.

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:20.510
Now when you get into,

00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:22.210
there are circumstances in the world

00:26:22.210 --> 00:26:25.860
where kids in some war-torn
place or refugee camp

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:27.590
where they don't have access to school,

00:26:27.590 --> 00:26:28.820
well those cases, yes,

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:31.780
online can be a very good
thing to keep people learning.

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:34.160
There's situations right
now because of COVID

00:26:34.160 --> 00:26:37.030
where certain school
systems haven't been able

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:39.420
to support their students
as much as others.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:41.930
Well there online learning obviously

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:44.450
is a very powerful way to
keep students learning.

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:48.010
And then as you get into almost adulthood

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:50.020
or being a young adult or an adult,

00:26:50.020 --> 00:26:53.420
that's where I think online
learning can be very powerful.

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:55.630
There's very powerful things
about going to college.

00:26:55.630 --> 00:26:57.040
I have very fond memories,

00:26:57.040 --> 00:26:59.230
once again a lot of the
socialization and all that,

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:03.280
but for some young adults
or even not-so-young-adults,

00:27:03.280 --> 00:27:04.587
they might say, "Hey, I have a job,

00:27:04.587 --> 00:27:06.396
"I have to support my family,
I need more flexibility,"

00:27:06.396 --> 00:27:09.630
and that's where online
learning can be super useful.

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:10.690
So simple answer is,

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:12.580
I hope that they can always work together,

00:27:12.580 --> 00:27:15.730
kind of a blended experience,
the best of both worlds.

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:18.780
That learning does not have
to be bound by time and space,

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:20.610
but some of the richest experiences

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:22.680
can happen in the physical environment,

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:24.790
but even there part of the richness comes

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:27.190
from the ability to leverage tools,

00:27:27.190 --> 00:27:29.763
online tools like Khan Academy.

00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:35.747
So Irada Irmo from YouTube is asking,

00:27:35.747 --> 00:27:37.667
"Can you talk about taking responsibility

00:27:37.667 --> 00:27:39.727
"such as becoming a
doctor but you don't have

00:27:39.727 --> 00:27:43.150
"the confidence or
consistent study habits?"

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:48.150
Well, Irada, I will just say
it's all about forming a habit.

00:27:49.670 --> 00:27:51.130
It's all about, you know,

00:27:51.130 --> 00:27:53.470
one of my good friends from
school's name is Charles Duhigg,

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:56.620
he wrote a very popular
book, "The Power of Habit."

00:27:56.620 --> 00:27:57.550
But you know there is science

00:27:57.550 --> 00:27:58.960
that habits tend to form over,

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:00.730
let's call it five, six, seven weeks,

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:03.710
and so what I would do
is, I would pick a goal.

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:05.860
Don't try to solve the
whole goal overnight,

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:07.160
that could be paralyzing,

00:28:07.160 --> 00:28:09.747
don't try to binge-study or cram, but say,

00:28:09.747 --> 00:28:13.270
"Hey, everyday I'm gonna
spend 20, 30, 40, 50

00:28:13.270 --> 00:28:16.390
minutes a day towards that goal."

00:28:16.390 --> 00:28:18.180
And if you do that for six or seven weeks,

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:19.370
you're gonna build that habit

00:28:19.370 --> 00:28:21.490
and then you're going to see the gains,

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:23.780
and that's going to help your confidence.

00:28:23.780 --> 00:28:25.880
I talked about growth mindset before.

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:28.040
Growth mindset is all about you don't say

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:29.770
I'm good at this or not good at this,

00:28:29.770 --> 00:28:31.447
I'm capable or not capable, you're like,

00:28:31.447 --> 00:28:33.397
"I just have to keep chipping away at it,

00:28:33.397 --> 00:28:35.467
"I have to keep stepping
out of my comfort zone,

00:28:35.467 --> 00:28:38.760
"if I get something wrong, if
I fail, I shouldn't give up."

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:41.170
That isn't somehow me saying,
"Oh, I'm no good at this."

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:42.037
That's when I should say,

00:28:42.037 --> 00:28:44.617
"Oh, awesome, I just learned
about my learning edge,

00:28:44.617 --> 00:28:47.527
"let me reflect on why I failed there

00:28:47.527 --> 00:28:50.030
"or why I got that wrong,
and let me keep trying."

00:28:50.030 --> 00:28:53.850
And you will be shocked, if
you just keep persevering,

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:55.920
eventually you will get
through those barriers,

00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:58.390
and you'll like, "Whoa, what
was the big deal there?"

00:28:58.390 --> 00:28:59.430
and I gotta tell you,

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:00.750
the biggest skill in life,

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:03.170
yes it's nice to have hard skills,

00:29:03.170 --> 00:29:04.950
to know how to factor a polynomial

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:08.470
or science or writing well,

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:11.200
but the most important thing in life

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.270
is that resiliency and
having that growth mindset.

00:29:14.270 --> 00:29:15.150
If you have that,

00:29:15.150 --> 00:29:18.610
and you're always just,
you keep in motion,

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:22.220
powering forward, then
frankly you'll be unstoppable.

00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:24.570
And I'll say another thing,
you know it's very tempting,

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:26.440
especially while you're in school,

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:28.920
where you're always
measured by test scores

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:30.010
and this and that,

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:31.970
and there's so much importance on them

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:33.297
that it's very easy to assume,

00:29:33.297 --> 00:29:35.577
"Oh, I keep getting a 95.

00:29:35.577 --> 00:29:40.447
"That person two desks
down always gets a 98.

00:29:40.447 --> 00:29:42.407
"They're always going to be better than me

00:29:42.407 --> 00:29:44.460
"because they always
score better on tests."

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:46.720
And what you see as soon as
you enter into the real world,

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:48.320
it doesn't work out that way.

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:50.290
The people who do well are the people

00:29:50.290 --> 00:29:52.330
with the growth mindsets
who don't give up,

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:54.910
who believe in themselves,
have that resilience.

00:29:54.910 --> 00:29:57.838
If I go back into my
own schooling and see,

00:29:57.838 --> 00:30:01.670
yes doing well in school
matters and it correlates,

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:03.690
but it's not like you should
just stack rank yourself

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:05.870
against other people and
say that's the ranking

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:06.960
that's going to be forever.

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:08.840
It has a lot more to do with mindset

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:11.480
than it has to do with
any fixed mindset notion

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:13.610
of quote innate ability

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:16.860
which frankly I don't really subscribe to.

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:19.050
So anyway, hopefully that helps,

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:21.500
and I realize that I'm all out of time,

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:25.570
so I just want to give a
special thanks to Conor Corey.

00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:28.450
Amazing teacher, amazing
teacher ambassador,

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:29.810
I think he had some great advice.

00:30:29.810 --> 00:30:32.500
Unfortunately, the sound was
clipping out a little bit,

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:33.805
but hopefully we'll be able to get him on

00:30:33.805 --> 00:30:35.910
in the not-too-far-off future.

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:37.490
But I just want to wish
everyone out there,

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:39.770
especially the amazing
teachers of the world,

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:42.170
Happy Teacher Appreciation Week.

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:44.780
You do amazing work all the time,

00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:47.930
and I think this week you're
continuing to do amazing work,

00:30:47.930 --> 00:30:49.800
or this time, COVID time,

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:52.580
I think many of us parents
have even higher appreciation,

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.700
so thank you for doing what you do.

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:57.600
There's no single higher leverage point

00:30:57.600 --> 00:30:59.690
for helping humanity, in my opinion,

00:30:59.690 --> 00:31:03.570
than your ability to empower
all of our collective children.

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:05.210
So with that, thank you so much,

00:31:05.210 --> 00:31:06.810
and I look forward to seeing all of y'all

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:08.383
at the live stream tomorrow.

